WEBVTT

00:00:03.147 --> 00:00:06.752
Start with one business outcome and the audience you
need to reach.

00:00:07.182 --> 00:00:12.473
Pick a channel you can support. Agree on what you’ll
measure, when you’ll review it, and when to stop.

00:00:13.272 --> 00:00:16.877
Review commitments across brands before promising
more work.

00:00:17.307 --> 00:00:26.608
Protect inbox coverage and client deadlines first. If the
team is stretched, defer a lower-value channel instead
of leaving every channel half covered.

00:00:27.397 --> 00:00:33.519
Routine comments get a helpful public answer. Move
personal details to the approved private route.

00:00:33.949 --> 00:00:40.456
For a complaint or risk, capture the context, name the
escalation owner, and make sure someone follows
through.

00:00:41.272 --> 00:00:46.733
Before recruiting a creator, check audience fit and
agree on the brief, rights, and disclosures.

00:00:47.163 --> 00:00:53.307
Run a small pilot. Scale only if the partnership leads to
the useful actions you agreed to measure.

00:00:54.105 --> 00:00:58.649
When an incident hits, pause affected scheduled
content and preserve the facts.

00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:06.012
Send the response to the right owner and approver.
Resume only after clearance is documented and the
queue is checked.

00:01:06.813 --> 00:01:10.653
Compare like with like, then trace reach to useful
audience actions.

00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:17.611
Bring one decision to the review: continue, iterate, or
stop. Write down the next test and who owns it.

00:01:18.397 --> 00:01:23.282
Record who owns each account, who can approve
work, and who can recover access.

00:01:23.712 --> 00:01:29.195
Give team members only the permissions required for
their role. Keep the client’s ownership clear.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:34.055
Start a brand or team handoff with a checklist, not a
login alone.

00:01:34.485 --> 00:01:41.653
At exit, transfer open work, remove access that’s no
longer needed, and verify the client still has control.
